\begin{abstract}
We study, over a finite outcome set, the two standard formulations of reward maximisation
under a relative-entropy regulariser: the \emph{penalised} problem
$\max_p \{\E_p[r] - \beta\,\KL(p\|\pi_0)\}$, whose solution is the exponential tilt
$\pi^*_\beta \propto \pi_0 e^{r/\beta}$, and the \emph{constrained} problem
$\max\{\E_p[r] : \KL(p\|\pi_0)\le k\}$. We prove their exact equivalence and, more precisely,
that $\beta \mapsto \KL(\pi^*_\beta\|\pi_0)$ is a bijection from $(0,\infty)$ onto an interval
$(0,L)$ whose right endpoint is the \emph{tropical ceiling}
$L = -\log \pi_0(\argmax r)$, the negative logarithm of the reference mass carried by the
reward-maximising outcomes. The whole development rests on a single algebraic identity,
\[
  t\bigl(\E_{p_t}[r]-\E_p[r]\bigr) = \KL(p_t\|\pi_0)-\KL(p\|\pi_0)+\KL(p\|p_t),
\]
and uses no differentiation of the log-partition function, no Lagrange multipliers and no KKT
theory. From it we deduce constrained optimality and uniqueness of the tilt inside its own
divergence ball; strict monotonicity and continuity of the budget curve
$k(t)=\KL(p_t\|\pi_0)$; convexity of $\log Z$ via a Bregman identity; a shadow-price bracket
identifying the temperature with the marginal price of one nat; concavity of the
reward--divergence Pareto frontier; and a two-sided tropical sandwich
$tM+\log w \le \log Z(t)\le tM$ exhibiting the zero-temperature limit as Maslov dequantization,
$t^{-1}\log Z(t)\to \max_i r(i)$. The frontier terminates at the tropical corner $(L,\max_i r)$.
\end{abstract}

\noindent\textbf{Keywords:} relative entropy, exponential tilting, Gibbs variational principle,
Maslov dequantization, tropical semiring, Bregman divergence, shadow price, alignment.

\section{Introduction}

\subsection{Two ways to say ``don't go too far''}

Let $\pi_0$ be a fixed reference probability distribution on a finite set of outcomes and let
$r$ assign a real-valued reward to each outcome. A pervasive design problem---appearing in
statistical mechanics, importance sampling, robust control, portfolio selection, large
deviations, and the reinforcement-learning fine-tuning of generative models---is to produce a
new distribution $p$ scoring highly under $r$ while remaining close to $\pi_0$ in relative
entropy. Two formulations compete:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Penalised (soft).} Maximise $J_\beta(p) = \E_p[r]-\beta\,\KL(p\|\pi_0)$ over
        probability vectors, for a fixed temperature $\beta>0$.
  \item \textbf{Constrained (hard).} Maximise $\E_p[r]$ over $\{p:\KL(p\|\pi_0)\le k\}$, for a
        fixed budget $k>0$.
\end{itemize}
Folklore holds these equivalent under a correspondence $\beta \leftrightarrow k$. The folklore
is correct, but standard derivations obscure two things. First, they invoke machinery far
heavier than the statement requires. Second, they rarely make the correspondence's
\emph{domain} explicit, and the domain is the interesting part: the achievable budgets form a
bounded interval whose endpoint is not an entropy but a max-plus invariant of $(\pi_0,r)$.

\subsection{Contributions}
\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{A calculus-free engine.} A single identity (Theorem~\ref{thm:duality}) implies
        constrained optimality, uniqueness, monotonicity of the budget curve, convexity of
        $\log Z$, the shadow-price bracket and concavity of the frontier.
  \item \textbf{The tropical ceiling.} The exact supremum of achievable budgets is
        $L=-\log w$, $w=\pi_0(\argmax r)$, and the budget curve is a strictly increasing
        continuous bijection $[0,\infty)\to[0,L)$.
  \item \textbf{Maslov dequantization with explicit error.} The sandwich
        $tM+\log w\le \log Z(t)\le tM$ both proves $t^{-1}\log Z(t)\to \max r$ and \emph{is}
        the source of the ceiling.
  \item \textbf{The headline equivalence} (Theorem~\ref{thm:main}).
  \item \textbf{The frontier}: Bregman identity, shadow-price bracket, concavity, and
        convergence to the tropical corner $(L,\max r)$.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Standing assumptions}
Throughout, $\iota$ is a finite nonempty index set of outcomes. A \emph{probability vector} is
$p:\iota\to\R$ with $p(i)\ge 0$ and $\sum_i p(i)=1$; write $\Delta$ for the set of these. The
reference $\pi_0$ is a probability vector with $\pi_0(i)>0$ for every $i$. The reward
$r:\iota\to\R$ is arbitrary; where strictness is needed we assume it \emph{non-constant}, i.e.
$r(i)\neq r(j)$ for some $i,j$.

\section{Definitions}

\begin{definition}[Relative entropy]
For $p,q:\iota\to\R$, $\KL(p\|q)=\sum_i p(i)\log\frac{p(i)}{q(i)}$, with the convention
$0\log 0=0$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Expectation]
$\E_p[f]=\sum_i p(i)f(i)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Partition function and tilt]
For $t\in\R$,
\[
  Z(t)=\sum_i \pi_0(i)e^{t r(i)},\qquad p_t(i)=\frac{\pi_0(i)e^{t r(i)}}{Z(t)} .
\]
We call $t$ the tilt parameter or inverse temperature. Since $\pi_0>0$ and $\iota\neq\emptyset$,
$Z(t)>0$ and $p_t\in\Delta$ with $p_t>0$ for every $t$; and $p_0=\pi_0$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Penalised optimum]
For $\beta>0$, $\pi^*_\beta := p_{1/\beta}$, i.e.\ $\pi^*_\beta(i)\propto \pi_0(i)e^{r(i)/\beta}$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Budget and value curves]
$k(t):=\KL(p_t\|\pi_0)$ and $V(t):=\E_{p_t}[r]$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Tropical data]
Let $M=\max_i r(i)$, $A=\{i: r(i)=M\}$, $w=\pi_0(A)=\sum_{i\in A}\pi_0(i)$. Define the
\emph{tropical ceiling} $L:=-\log w$ and the \emph{zero-temperature policy}
$p_\infty(i)=\pi_0(i)/w$ for $i\in A$ and $0$ otherwise.
\end{definition}

Here $M$ is the value of $r$ in the max-plus semiring $(\R\cup\{-\infty\},\max,+)$, $A$ is its
tropical support, and $0<w\le 1$, so $L\ge 0$ with $L=0$ iff $\pi_0$ is supported on $A$.

\section{The engine: Gibbs, decomposition, duality}

\begin{lemma}[Termwise Gibbs bound]\label{lem:gibbsterm}
For $a\ge 0$ and $b>0$, $a-b\le a\log(a/b)$, with equality iff $a=b$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
For $a=0$ the claim reads $-b\le 0$, strict. For $a>0$, apply $\log x\le x-1$ (strict for
$x\neq 1$) at $x=b/a$: $-\log(a/b)\le b/a-1$; multiply by $a>0$ and rearrange. Strictness
survives because $b/a\neq 1$ exactly when $a\neq b$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Gibbs' inequality with equality case]\label{thm:gibbs}
If $p,q\in\Delta$ and $q>0$ pointwise then $\KL(p\|q)\ge 0$, with equality iff $p=q$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Summing Lemma~\ref{lem:gibbsterm} termwise gives $\sum_i (p(i)-q(i))\le \KL(p\|q)$, and the left
side is $1-1=0$. If $p(i_0)\neq q(i_0)$ for some $i_0$ the corresponding term is strict, so a
strict sum comparison gives $\KL(p\|q)>0$. Conversely $p=q$ makes every summand zero.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Master decomposition]\label{thm:master}
For every $p\in\Delta$ and every $t\in\R$,
\[
  \KL(p\|p_t)=\KL(p\|\pi_0)-t\,\E_p[r]+\log Z(t).
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Pointwise $\log p_t(i)=\log\pi_0(i)+t r(i)-\log Z(t)$. Hence for each $i$ with $p(i)>0$,
$p(i)\log\frac{p(i)}{p_t(i)}=p(i)\log\frac{p(i)}{\pi_0(i)}-t\,p(i)r(i)+p(i)\log Z(t)$, while for
$p(i)=0$ both sides vanish. Sum over $i$, using $\sum_i p(i)=1$ on the last term.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Closed form for the budget]\label{cor:kclosed}
$k(t)=t\,V(t)-\log Z(t)$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Take $p=p_t$ in Theorem~\ref{thm:master}; the left side vanishes by Theorem~\ref{thm:gibbs}.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[The duality identity]\label{thm:duality}
For every $p\in\Delta$ and every $t\in\R$,
\[
  t\bigl(V(t)-\E_p[r]\bigr)=k(t)-\KL(p\|\pi_0)+\KL(p\|p_t).
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Subtract Corollary~\ref{cor:kclosed} from Theorem~\ref{thm:master}; $\log Z(t)$ cancels.
\end{proof}

\section{Constrained optimality of the tilt}

\begin{theorem}[The tilt maximises reward in its own ball]\label{thm:opt}
Let $t>0$ and $p\in\Delta$ with $\KL(p\|\pi_0)\le k(t)$. Then $\E_p[r]\le V(t)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
In Theorem~\ref{thm:duality} the right-hand side is a sum of two nonnegative terms: the first by
feasibility, the second by Theorem~\ref{thm:gibbs}. Hence $t(V(t)-\E_p[r])\ge 0$ and $t>0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Uniqueness of the maximiser]\label{thm:uniqopt}
If moreover $\E_p[r]=V(t)$ then $p=p_t$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The left-hand side of Theorem~\ref{thm:duality} is $0$, so both nonnegative terms vanish; in
particular $\KL(p\|p_t)=0$, whence $p=p_t$ by Theorem~\ref{thm:gibbs}.
\end{proof}

Rearranging Theorem~\ref{thm:master} gives, for $\beta=1/t$,
$\E_p[r]-\beta\KL(p\|\pi_0)=\beta\log Z(1/\beta)-\beta\KL(p\|p_t)$, i.e.\ the Gibbs variational
principle: the penalised objective is maximised exactly at $p=\pi^*_\beta$, with optimal value
$\beta\log Z(1/\beta)$.

\section{The budget curve and the tropical ceiling}

\begin{theorem}[Jeffreys identity]\label{thm:jeffreys}
For all $s,t\in\R$,
$(t-s)\bigl(V(t)-V(s)\bigr)=\KL(p_s\|p_t)+\KL(p_t\|p_s)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Apply Theorem~\ref{thm:duality} with $p=p_s$ at parameter $t$, and with $p=p_t$ at parameter
$s$; add. The budget terms cancel.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Value monotonicity]\label{cor:vmono}
$V$ is nondecreasing on $\R$: colder tilts never score worse.
\end{corollary}

\begin{lemma}[Quantitative budget monotonicity]\label{lem:kmono}
For $0\le s\le t$, $k(t)-k(s)\ge \KL(p_t\|p_s)\ge 0$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Theorem~\ref{thm:duality} with $p=p_t$ at parameter $s$ reads
$s(V(s)-V(t))=k(s)-k(t)+\KL(p_t\|p_s)$. By Corollary~\ref{cor:vmono} and $s\ge0$ the left side
is $\le 0$; rearrange.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Injectivity of the tilt family]\label{lem:inj}
If $s\neq t$ and $p_s=p_t$ then $r$ is constant.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Taking logarithms in $p_s(i)=p_t(i)$ gives $t r(i)-\log Z(t)=s r(i)-\log Z(s)$ for every $i$, so
$(t-s)r(i)$ is independent of $i$; cancel $t-s\neq 0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Strict monotonicity of the budget curve]\label{thm:strictmono}
If $r$ is non-constant then $k$ is strictly increasing on $[0,\infty)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
For $0\le s<t$, Lemma~\ref{lem:inj} gives $p_t\neq p_s$, hence $\KL(p_t\|p_s)>0$ by the equality
case of Theorem~\ref{thm:gibbs}; apply Lemma~\ref{lem:kmono}.
\end{proof}

Moreover $k(0)=0$ and $k$ is continuous on $\R$ (each $p_t(i)$ is a continuous function of $t$
and $x\mapsto x\log x$ is continuous on $[0,\infty)$), so $k\ge 0$ on $[0,\infty)$.

\begin{theorem}[Tropical sandwich]\label{thm:sandwich}
For all $t\ge 0$: $\;t M+\log w \le \log Z(t)\le t M$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Upper: $r(i)\le M$ and $t\ge 0$ give $Z(t)\le \sum_i \pi_0(i)e^{tM}=e^{tM}$. Lower: restricting
the (positive-term) sum to $A$, on which $r\equiv M$, gives $Z(t)\ge w e^{tM}$. Take logarithms.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Maslov dequantization]\label{thm:maslov}
$\displaystyle\lim_{t\to\infty}\frac{\log Z(t)}{t}=M=\max_i r(i)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Divide Theorem~\ref{thm:sandwich} by $t>0$ and squeeze.
\end{proof}

Equivalently $\beta\log Z(1/\beta)\to \max_i r(i)$ as $\beta\downarrow 0$: the dequantization of
$(\R_{>0},+,\times)$ into $(\R\cup\{-\infty\},\max,+)$. The error is bounded by the single
constant $|\log w| = L$, uniformly in $t\ge 0$.

\begin{theorem}[Hard cap on the budget]\label{thm:cap}
For all $t\ge 0$, $k(t)\le L$; if $r$ is non-constant then $k(t)<L$ for all $t\ge0$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$\E_p[r]\le M$ for every $p\in\Delta$, so by Corollary~\ref{cor:kclosed} and the lower half of
Theorem~\ref{thm:sandwich}, $k(t)=tV(t)-\log Z(t)\le tM-(tM+\log w)=-\log w=L$. For strictness
with non-constant $r$: at $t=0$, $k(0)=0<L$; for $t>0$, $p_t$ places positive mass off $A$, so
$V(t)<M$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[The ceiling is attained in the limit]\label{thm:ceil}
$\displaystyle\lim_{t\to\infty}k(t)=L$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Since $\pi_0>0$, write $\KL(p\|\pi_0)=\sum_i\bigl(p(i)\log p(i)-p(i)\log\pi_0(i)\bigr)$. Dividing
numerator and denominator of $p_t(i)$ by $e^{tM}$ gives
$p_t(i)=\pi_0(i)e^{t(r(i)-M)}/\sum_j\pi_0(j)e^{t(r(j)-M)}$, where each exponent is $\le0$ and
vanishes exactly on $A$; hence $p_t(i)\to p_\infty(i)$. Continuity of $x\mapsto x\log x$ on
$[0,\infty)$ lets the entropy form pass to the limit termwise, so $k(t)\to\KL(p_\infty\|\pi_0)$,
and
$\KL(p_\infty\|\pi_0)=\sum_{i\in A}\frac{\pi_0(i)}{w}\log\frac1w=-\log w=L$.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}[What the ceiling is, and is not]\label{rem:ceiling}
$L$ depends on $r$ only through its argmax \emph{set}: replacing $r$ by $\lambda r+c$ with
$\lambda>0$ leaves $L$ unchanged. It is not the entropy of $\pi_0$ and not a bound on the reward
scale; it is the negative log-mass of the tropical support, a purely max-plus invariant. If
$\pi_0$ is uniform on $n$ outcomes with a unique maximiser, $L=\log n$.
\end{remark}

\section{Constrained equals penalised}

\begin{theorem}[Unique tilt for a prescribed budget]\label{thm:uniqtilt}
Assume $r$ non-constant. For every $k$ with $0\le k<L$ there is exactly one $t\ge 0$ with
$k(t)=k$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Existence: by Theorem~\ref{thm:ceil} there is $T\ge 0$ with $k(T)>k$; $k$ is continuous on
$[0,T]$ with $k(0)=0\le k\le k(T)$, so the intermediate value theorem applies. Uniqueness:
Theorem~\ref{thm:strictmono}.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Unique temperature]\label{thm:uniqbeta}
Assume $r$ non-constant. For every $k$ with $0<k<L$ there is exactly one $\beta>0$ with
$\KL(\pi^*_\beta\|\pi_0)=k$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Take $t$ from Theorem~\ref{thm:uniqtilt}; $t>0$ since $k(0)=0\neq k$. Put $\beta=1/t$. Any other
admissible $\beta'$ yields $1/\beta'\ge0$ realising the same budget, hence $1/\beta'=t$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Constrained $=$ penalised]\label{thm:main}
Let $\pi_0>0$ be a probability vector on a finite set, $r$ non-constant, and
$L=-\log \pi_0(\argmax r)$. For every budget $k$ with $0<k<L$ there is a \emph{unique}
$\beta>0$ such that
\begin{enumerate}
  \item $\KL(\pi^*_\beta\|\pi_0)=k$;
  \item $\E_{\pi^*_\beta}[r]=\max\{\E_p[r] : p\in\Delta,\ \KL(p\|\pi_0)\le k\}$, the maximum
        being attained;
  \item any $p\in\Delta$ with $\KL(p\|\pi_0)\le k$ and $\E_p[r]=\E_{\pi^*_\beta}[r]$ equals
        $\pi^*_\beta$.
\end{enumerate}
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Theorem~\ref{thm:uniqbeta} gives the unique $\beta$ realising the budget. With $t=1/\beta>0$,
item 2 is Theorem~\ref{thm:opt} together with feasibility of $\pi^*_\beta$, and item 3 is
Theorem~\ref{thm:uniqopt}. Uniqueness of $\beta$ follows from item 1 alone.
\end{proof}

\begin{example}[Two outcomes]
Take $\iota=\{0,1\}$, $\pi_0=(\tfrac12,\tfrac12)$, $r=(0,1)$. Then $M=1$, $A=\{1\}$,
$w=\tfrac12$, so $L=\log 2\approx 0.6931$. With $t=1/\beta$ and $\sigma=e^t/(1+e^t)$,
\[
  \pi^*_\beta=(1-\sigma,\ \sigma),\qquad
  k(t)=\log 2+\sigma\log\sigma+(1-\sigma)\log(1-\sigma),\qquad V(t)=\sigma,
\]
and as $t$ runs over $[0,\infty)$, $k$ increases bijectively from $0$ to $\log 2$. The
hypotheses of Theorem~\ref{thm:main} are therefore satisfiable and its conclusion non-vacuous.
\end{example}

\begin{remark}[Failure above the ceiling]
For $k\ge L$ no tilt realises the budget: the supremum of the constrained problem is $M$, and it
is achieved by the zero-temperature policy $p_\infty$, an object of max-plus rather than
exponential type. The ceiling is the exact phase boundary between the exponential-family regime
and the tropical regime.
\end{remark}

\section{The reward--divergence frontier}

Consider $\Gamma: t\mapsto (k(t),V(t))$, $t\in[0,\infty)$. By Theorem~\ref{thm:main} the second
coordinate is the constrained value function evaluated at the first.

\begin{theorem}[Bregman identity]\label{thm:bregman}
For all $s,t$: $\KL(p_s\|p_t)=\log Z(t)-\log Z(s)-(t-s)V(s)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Theorem~\ref{thm:master} with $p=p_s$ gives $\KL(p_s\|p_t)=k(s)-tV(s)+\log Z(t)$; substitute
$k(s)=sV(s)-\log Z(s)$ from Corollary~\ref{cor:kclosed}.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Supporting lines and convexity]\label{cor:convex}
$\log Z(s)+(t-s)V(s)\le \log Z(t)$ for all $s,t$; consequently $\log Z$ is convex on $\R$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The inequality is Theorem~\ref{thm:bregman} plus $\KL\ge0$. For convexity let $m=ax+by$ with
$a,b\ge0$, $a+b=1$; combine the supporting-line inequality at $s=m$ with $t=x$ and with $t=y$,
weighted by $a$ and $b$. The slope terms carry the factor $a(x-m)+b(y-m)=0$, leaving
$\log Z(m)\le a\log Z(x)+b\log Z(y)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Shadow-price bracket]\label{thm:shadow}
For $0<s\le t$,
\[
  s\bigl(V(t)-V(s)\bigr)\ \le\ k(t)-k(s)\ \le\ t\bigl(V(t)-V(s)\bigr);
\]
equivalently, whenever $k(t)>k(s)$, $\ \frac1t\le \frac{V(t)-V(s)}{k(t)-k(s)}\le \frac1s$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Theorem~\ref{thm:duality} with $p=p_s$ at $t$ gives $t(V(t)-V(s))=k(t)-k(s)+\KL(p_s\|p_t)$; with
$p=p_t$ at $s$ it gives $s(V(s)-V(t))=k(s)-k(t)+\KL(p_t\|p_s)$. Drop the nonnegative divergences.
\end{proof}

Thus the temperature is the shadow price of the divergence constraint: every chord slope of the
frontier between tilt parameters $s$ and $t$ lies between $\beta_t=1/t$ and $\beta_s=1/s$.

\begin{theorem}[Concavity of the frontier]\label{thm:concave}
For $0<s\le t\le u$,
\[
  \bigl(k(u)-k(t)\bigr)\bigl(V(t)-V(s)\bigr)\ \ge\ \bigl(k(t)-k(s)\bigr)\bigl(V(u)-V(t)\bigr).
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
By Theorem~\ref{thm:shadow}, $k(t)-k(s)\le t(V(t)-V(s))$ and $k(u)-k(t)\ge t(V(u)-V(t))$; both
budget increments are nonnegative by Lemma~\ref{lem:kmono}. Cross-multiply through $t>0$.
\end{proof}

So the chord slopes $\Delta V/\Delta k$ decrease as the budget grows: diminishing returns are a
theorem, not an empirical regularity.

\begin{theorem}[Sublinear budget growth and the tropical corner]\label{thm:corner}
$k(t)/t\to 0$, $V(t)\to M$, and $\Gamma(t)\to (L,M)$ as $t\to\infty$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$0\le k(t)\le L$ by Theorem~\ref{thm:cap}, so $k(t)/t\to0$. By Corollary~\ref{cor:kclosed},
$V(t)=k(t)/t+\log Z(t)/t$ for $t>0$, and Theorem~\ref{thm:maslov} handles the second term.
Combine with Theorem~\ref{thm:ceil}.
\end{proof}

The frontier is therefore a concave arc from $(0,\E_{\pi_0}[r])$ to the tropical corner $(L,M)$,
each point realised by exactly one temperature.

\section{Algorithms}

Let $n=|\iota|$.

\paragraph{(A) Budget evaluation, $O(n)$.} Given $t$, set $m=\max_i t r(i)$,
$u_i=\pi_0(i)e^{t r(i)-m}$, $S=\sum_i u_i$, $p_t=u/S$, $V=\sum_i p_t(i)r(i)$ and
$k=tV-(m+\log S)$ (Corollary~\ref{cor:kclosed}); subtracting $m$ prevents overflow.

\paragraph{(B) Temperature calibration, $O(n\log(1/\varepsilon))$.} Given $k\in(0,L)$, find the
unique $t$ with $k(t)=k$: double an upper bracket until $k(t_{\mathrm{hi}})>k$ (which terminates
by Theorem~\ref{thm:ceil} exactly because $k<L$), then bisect, using strict monotonicity
(Theorem~\ref{thm:strictmono}). Return $\beta=1/t$, which by Theorem~\ref{thm:shadow} is the
marginal price of a nat at that budget.

\paragraph{(C) Ceiling computation, $O(n)$.}
$L=-\log\sum_{i: r(i)=\max r}\pi_0(i)$, determining a priori which budgets are feasible.

\section{Applications and discussion}

\paragraph{Alignment of generative models.} The penalised objective with $\pi_0$ a base model
and $r$ a learned reward is the standard fine-tuning target, yet practitioners reason in terms
of a ``KL budget''. Theorem~\ref{thm:main} licenses that translation exactly, and
Theorem~\ref{thm:shadow} says the tuned $\beta$ \emph{is} the reward-per-nat exchange rate at
the operating point. Remark~\ref{rem:ceiling} adds a caution: achievable budgets are bounded by
$-\log \pi_0(\argmax r)$, fixed by the base model and the argmax set alone.

\paragraph{Statistical mechanics.} With $r=-E$ and $\beta$ the temperature, $p_t$ is the
Boltzmann distribution, $\log Z$ the free energy, Theorem~\ref{thm:master} the free-energy
variational principle, Corollary~\ref{cor:convex} its convexity, and Theorem~\ref{thm:corner}
the zero-temperature limit onto the ground-state manifold, with $L$ the residual entropy of the
ground states relative to the density of states.

\paragraph{Large deviations.} Theorem~\ref{thm:main} is a finite, non-asymptotic sibling of the
Gibbs conditioning principle: conditioning on a rare high-reward event is equivalent to tilting,
and the divergence budget is the rate.

\paragraph{Tropical geometry.} The $\beta\to0$ limit is Maslov dequantization; the max-plus value
$M$, the tropical support $A$ and its reference mass $w$ are the surviving invariants.
Theorem~\ref{thm:sandwich} quantifies the dequantization with uniform error $L$, and that same
error becomes the ceiling.

\paragraph{Robust control and risk.} $\beta\log Z(1/\beta)$ is the entropic risk measure;
Theorem~\ref{thm:main} recasts it as the value of a divergence-ball robust optimisation.

\paragraph{What the calculus-free route buys.} Standard treatments differentiate $\log Z$ to get
$\frac{d}{dt}\log Z=V(t)$ and $\frac{d}{dt}k(t)=t\Var_{p_t}(r)$, reading monotonicity off the
variance. That needs differentiation under the sum and does not transfer painlessly to infinite
alphabets. Here monotonicity, optimality, convexity and concavity all reduce to nonnegativity of
relative entropy, which survives in any measure-theoretic setting.

\section{Limitations and future work}

Finiteness is used to guarantee that $\max r$ is attained and $Z(t)<\infty$; strict positivity of
$\pi_0$ to guarantee finiteness of every divergence; non-constancy of $r$ only for strictness.

\begin{conjecture}[Tropical ceiling rigidity for countable alphabets]
For a reference measure $\pi_0$ on a countably infinite alphabet with $\sup_i r(i)=M<\infty$,
the achievable divergence range is $[0,L)$ with $L=-\log\pi_0(\argmax r)$ \emph{iff} the argmax
set has positive $\pi_0$-mass; if $\pi_0(\argmax r)=0$ (in particular if the supremum is not
attained) the range is all of $[0,\infty)$ and no finite ceiling exists.
\end{conjecture}
The ceiling is not an entropy bound but the negative log-mass of the tropical support, and it
should degenerate exactly when the argmax cell becomes null. The finite proof uses only the
sandwich $Z(t)\in[we^{tM},e^{tM}]$ and dominated pointwise convergence of the tilt, both of which
have measure-theoretic analogues. A natural falsifying test: $\pi_0$ geometric with
$r(i)=1-2^{-i}$.

\begin{conjecture}[Sharp alignment tax]
For every $0<k<L$ the constrained value function
$V(k)=\max\{\E_p[r]:\KL(p\|\pi_0)\le k\}$ satisfies
\[
  \E_{\pi_0}[r]+\frac{k}{t(k)}\ \le\ V(k)\ \le\ \E_{\pi_0}[r]+\bigl(\max r-\E_{\pi_0}[r]\bigr)
    \bigl(1-e^{-k}\bigr),
\]
where $t(k)$ is the unique tilt realising $k$; in particular the marginal price $dV/dk=1/t(k)$
decreases from a $\Var_{\pi_0}(r)^{1/2}$-scale at $k\to0$ to $0$ at $k\to L$.
\end{conjecture}
The shadow-price bracket already brackets every chord slope of the frontier, so the conjecture
amounts to integrating that bracket and controlling the endpoints.

Further directions: multiple constraints and vector rewards, where the ceiling should become the
negative log-mass of a tropical polyhedral cell; estimation and sampling, where the bracket
suggests estimating slopes rather than budgets; and the question of which results survive for
general $f$-divergences---the master decomposition is special to relative entropy, but the
shadow-price bracket, being order-theoretic, may generalise.

\section{Conclusion}

A single identity,
\[
  t\bigl(\E_{p_t}[r]-\E_p[r]\bigr)=\KL(p_t\|\pi_0)-\KL(p\|\pi_0)+\KL(p\|p_t),
\]
suffices to prove that constrained and penalised reward maximisation under a relative-entropy
regulariser are the same problem, with a bijection between temperatures $\beta\in(0,\infty)$ and
budgets $k\in(0,L)$; that the temperature is the marginal price of a nat; that the
reward--divergence frontier is concave; and that the feasible region terminates at the tropical
corner $(L,\max r)$ with $L=-\log\pi_0(\argmax r)$. The constant measuring how well max-plus
arithmetic approximates ordinary arithmetic is exactly the constant measuring how far a policy
can ever be driven from its origin.

